%U A397318 1,8,13,9,1,6,1,17,6,9,3,9,1,9,12,19,1,6,9,20,17,10,1,6,5,11,18,22,11,7,1,18,13,12
%N A397318 a(n) is the number of positive integers that are less than or equal to and relatively prime to exactly two divisors of n.
%C A397318 There is a beautiful identity: Sum_{d|n} phi(d) = n, where phi is the Euler totient function.
%C A397318 We can rewrite this identity as the cardinality of a set of pairs. Define P(n)={(d,x) in N^2 : d|n, 1<=x<=n, gcd(x,n)=1} and we have that |P(n)| = n. For example, |P(6)| = |{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(3,2),(6,1),(6,5)}| = 6.
%C A397318 We might be interested in the frequencies of a certain x value of P(n). Let r(n,y)=|R(n,y)| where R(n,y)={(d,x) in P(n) : x=y}. For example, r(6,1) = |{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(6,1)}| = 4 while r(6,2)=r(6,5)=1 and for other x values r(6,x) = 0. This gives yet another reformulation of the identity: Sum_{x=1..n} r(n,x) = n. For example, Sum_{x=1..6} r(6,x) = 4+1+0+0+1+0 = 6. On the other hand Sum_{x=1..7} r(7,x) = 2+1+1+1+1+1+0 = 7.
%C A397318 This sequence counts the number of 2's in each such sum. Define a(n)=|Q(n,2)| where Q(n,y) = {x in [1,n] : r(n,x)=y}; that is, Q(n,y)={x in N : |{d in N : d|n, x<=d and gcd(x,d)=1}|=y} or specifically Q(n,2) = {x in N : there are exactly two d such that d|n, x<=d and gcd(x,d)=1}. For example from above we see a(6) = |Q(6,2)| = |{}| = 0, while a(7) = |Q(7,2)| = |{1}| = 1.
%H A397318 Eric Hogle, <a href="/A397318/b397318.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..500</a>
%e A397318 a(36) = |{2,13,17}| = 3. Each of these three numbers is less than or equal to and coprime with exactly two divisors of 36: We have 2<3 and 2<9 but less than no other coprime divisors, 13<18 and 13<36 but greater than other coprime divisors, and likewise 17<18 and 17<36.
%e A397318 These reflect the three 2s appearing in the sum 36 = Sum_{x=1..36} r(36,x) = 9+2+1+1+5+0+4+1+0+0+3+0+2+0+0+0+2+0+1+0+0+0+1+0+1+0+0+0+1+0+1+0+0+0+1+0.
%o A397318 (Python)
%o A397318 from math import gcd
%o A397318 def a(n):
%o A397318     divs=[d for d in range(1, n+1) if n%d==0]
%o A397318     Qn2=[]
%o A397318     for x in range(1, n+1):
%o A397318         Rnx=[d for d in divs if gcd(x, d)==1 and x<=d]
%o A397318         if len(Rnx)==2:
%o A397318             Qn2.append(x)
%o A397318     return len(Qn2)
%Y A397318 Cf. A000010 (Euler's totient), A398171 (for one divisor), A397786 (for k divisors).
